  "image_name": "3a250d28384c4f01825dcbc6dc4df14c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how charge carriers in a crystal can become localized and form polarons through coupling to lattice distortions. The left side shows a regular lattice that is noticeably deformed around an injected charge at the center: nearby atoms move inward and outward, indicating a local structural relaxation rather than a uniform distortion of the whole crystal.\n\nThe lower-left images give concrete examples for a perovskite-like oxide lattice, showing a self-trapped electron and a self-trapped hole. In both cases, the charge density is concentrated on a small region and accompanied by local atomic displacements, illustrating carrier localization.\n\nThe energy diagrams compare delocalized and localized electronic states as a function of lattice distortion. A delocalized electron is favored in the undistorted lattice, but when a polaronic distortion develops, the localized state can become lower in energy. The sketches on the right reinforce this contrast: the conduction electron is spread over many sites, whereas the polaron is compact and surrounded by local distortions.\n\nThe bottom-right diagram presents hopping between two equivalent sites. Two energy wells along a reaction coordinate are separated by a barrier and weak coupling, depicting thermally activated polaron motion from Site 1 to Site 2.",
